From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 01:33:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: overlap prompt chunk pairs across the AFD
 wire (one chunk's attention during the other's experts)

TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS=1 on the AFD attention node runs two consecutive prompt chunks through the layers together while
no stream decodes: chunk k's MoE exchange of layer L goes out, chunk k + 1's attention of layer L runs while the expert
nodes compute k's, k's reply comes in, k + 1's exchange goes out, k's attention of layer L + 1 runs while they compute
k + 1's, and so on. Unset or 0 (the default) keeps the schedule and every message as before.

Exact by construction: each chunk keeps its rows, its row count and its kernels; only the order of the two chunks'
kernels changes (attention(k + 1, L) after attention(k, L), whose latents, indexer keys and KDA state it reads;
attention(k, L + 1) after k's layer-L reply). Each MoE exchange keeps its rows, so the experts' grouping and every bit
are the chunks' alone.

- prefill_pairs.py: the pair scheduler (each chunk's layer loop stops before its exchange, before its collect and
  after it; one request on the wire at a time), the second chunk's own buffers (token ids, residual streams,
  hyper-connection weights, DFlash2 taps: 144 MiB at 2,048 rows), its view of a stream continuing from the first
  chunk (At), kept states at the first chunk's end taken layer by layer (Hold), each chunk's taps and commits in order.
- forward.py: layer_forward as layer_front, the FFN block and hc_post; moe_block as moe_route, moe_send (the wire out,
  then the shared expert) and the wire's collect. The same calls in the same order as before.
- afd.py: MODE_PAIR = 7 beside the slice wire's MODE_SLICE = 5 (the wire version stays 2); begin_pair writes both
  windows' headers (words 7 slot, 8 the other window's rows, 9 the pair's first seq); exchange / collect refuse a
  reply out of order or a second request on the wire; a pair that stops early is refused at the next header;
  TF_AFD_CHECK checks paired forwards and names them; TF_AFD_TIME samples each paired exchange's window, wait and
  in-window work and prints them by window size every 8 pairs. settings_for adds "pairs": 1 only with the setting on.
- afd_mcdma.py / experts_node.py: the expert takes a pair's second window after the first's layer-0 reply and serves
  the windows' layers in arrival order; MODE_PAIR only when the settings handshake carried "pairs" (an older expert
  refuses the settings, and any mode it does not know). A pair's header tables carry no slice count, and a slice
  after a pair no pair words.
- multi.py: rank 0 at world 1 pairs this iteration's chunk with the one the next would fill, when no stream decodes,
  no layer-sliced chunk (TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS) is suspended, the first chunk ends no prompt and no stream drafts with
  the MTP head (TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL's grouped chunks pair too); a chunk started while others decode still goes in
  layer slices, unpaired. /health's prefill_pairs counters. decode.take_snapshot takes held KDA states. engine.py:
  the setting read before the wire opens, the second chunk's buffers and their bytes in the capacity plan.

Tests: tests/test_glm_prefill_pairs.py (each chunk's recorded kernel calls in a pair equal its solo chunk's, dense, at
and past a dense limit, odd sizes, grouped chunks; the schedule interleaves; the real scheduler over the real wire and
the fake MCDMA pair, CPU- and GPU-driven; pair headers, out-of-order and second requests refused, an old peer refused,
a pair stopped early, a stalled link, the check's names, the pair timer line; pairs and layer slices on one wire in
either order; at world 1, the setting on equals off in replies, cached counts, kept states and their pool rows for
prompts of 1, 2, 3 and 5 chunks and odd remainders, with TF_GLM_SHARED_PREFIX, TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL and fp8 rings, a
cancellation after a pair and a client leaving mid-pair, no pair while a stream decodes, and the sliced fills unchanged
with the setting on); tests/test_glm_multi_afd.py's afd_fake runs pairs on the fake model, so its world-1 tests also
run with the setting on; tests/test_glm_sliced_fill.py's checked wire logs a pair and checks its chunks as whole-chunk
forwards, so its world-1 tests run with the setting on too.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

